<script data-rh="true" id="seo-schema-0" type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Partial Sale vs. Full Sale: How Much of Your Catalog Should You Sell?","description":"Compare selling a partial interest in your music catalog versus selling 100% of your rights. Understand the strategic, financial, and creative implications of each approach.","url":"https://spacemusic.io/compare/partial-sale-vs-full-sale/"}</script>
<script data-rh="true" id="seo-schema-1" type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How does pricing work for a partial sale vs. a full sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A partial sale is typically priced as a proportional fraction of the full catalog value, sometimes with a minority discount (5-15%) since the buyer doesn't get full control. A full sale may include a control premium. For example, if a catalog is worth $100,000, a 50% share might sell for $45,000-$50,000 rather than exactly $50,000."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I buy back the sold portion later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"This is possible if your purchase agreement includes a buyback option or right of first refusal. Without such provisions, you would need to negotiate a repurchase from the current owner at the then-current market value, which may be higher than what you originally sold it for."}}]}</script>
